@* FALLBACK ROUTE — <Router> matches the requested URL against every @page route in the
   assembly, and <Found> renders the page that matched.
   There is deliberately NO <NotFound> template here. In a Blazor Web App it compiles and
   never fires — not on the server pass, not on the interactive pass. It is dead code kept
   only for backward compatibility, which is exactly what makes it a trap.
   NotFoundPage is what replaces it, and it does TWO jobs:
     1. The interactive pass of a BAD URL. The server pass already rendered NotFound.razor
        (UseStatusCodePagesWithReExecute in Program.cs). But once the Blazor circuit connects,
        the router re-runs against the URL the user actually typed (/nonexistent) — which still
        matches nothing. NotFoundPage is what it renders. Without it, Blazor's built-in bare
        "Not found" text overwrites the styled page a moment after it appears.
     2. NavigationManager.NotFound() — a URL that DID match a route, but whose data does not
        exist (see ProductDetail.razor, /products/999).
   The middleware and NotFoundPage are BOTH required for a bad URL. See blazor-gotchas.md 6.5. *@
<Router AppAssembly="typeof(Program).Assembly" NotFoundPage="typeof(Pages.NotFound)">
    <Found Context="routeData">
        <RouteView RouteData="routeData" DefaultLayout="typeof(Layout.MainLayout)" />
        <FocusOnNavigate RouteData="routeData" Selector="h1" />
    </Found>
</Router>
